-----------------------------------------------------------------------
THE EIGHT CASES
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  case  h       field    edge buffer   .stor wrong-sign coefs
  01    0.025   uniform  off           reported
  02    0.025   uniform  1.0 h         HARD: none
  03    0.025   uniform  0.5 h         reported (see FINDINGS)
  04    0.0125  uniform  2.0 h         HARD: none
  05    0.025   radial   off           reported
  06    0.025   radial   1.0 h         HARD: none
  07    0.0125  radial   1.0 h         HARD: none
  08    0.008   radial   1.0 h         HARD: none

  radial: h = max(floor, 0.1 x distance from the axis x = y = 0.5),
  floor = H_REFINE_SIZE. In the reduced box r reaches 0.42, so h tops
  out at 0.042; the ceiling (0.075) is never reached. The checker
  carries its own copy of this formula
  (make_field), because the driver releases the field mesh.

  Edge buffer on: the sampler fills the box shrunk by buf x h, then the
  driver adds a regular lattice of points on the six faces.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
THE BOX
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  The only edit made to the deck: POI_XMIN..POI_YMAX changed from
  [0, 1] to [0.2, 0.8] (Oct 6 2026). Run time scales with the box
  volume through the lookup mesh, the face brick and the point count,
  so this cuts it to roughly 40 %.

  Kept: z = [0, 0.3] (a thinner box would leave the 2 h buffer case
  almost no interior), and the box centred on x = y = 0.5, where
  test_user_resolution.mlgi hard-codes the h field axis.

  Lost: the radial cases grade over a smaller range (h up to 0.042,
  was 0.071). To restore it, raise SLOPE from 0.1 to about 0.17 in
  the deck AND every slope= entry in test_Poisson_3D.cases.

  Measured Oct 6: LaGriT 128 s -> 44 s; 251 008 -> 117 157 nodes.
  Every row still PASSes and the original box's findings all still
  show: wrong-sign .stor pairs 01 165, 03 1082, 05 149, others 0
  (buffer 0.5 h still the worst); interior pairs down to 0.65-0.82 h;
  slivers in every case (smallest dihedral 0.095 deg, case 08).

  Side effect: in floating point 0.8 - 0.2 = 0.6000000000000001, so
  ceiling(L/h) gains one point and the face lattice is exactly h apart
  in x and y. The off-by-one of finding 2 now shows along z only.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
RESULTS
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  Rows: run, deck, case_01 .. case_08, merge, field.

  PASS   every HARD check holds
  SKIP   field row only: no field dump to check (the default)
  FAIL   anything else. The exit status is nonzero on any FAIL.

  HARD checks (exact definitions in check_test_poisson_3d.py):

  run     lagrit exit status 0; one createpts echo and one driver exit
          line per case in lagrit.out; no bare ERROR/WARNING line; on
          stdout, no POISSON DISK error/warning block and a clean
          "poisson_disk_3d exit" per case
  deck    every line of test_Poisson_3D.cases matches the defines the
          deck has in effect at that infile (catches drift)
  case    .inp and .stor written and readable; tets only; .stor has
          one row per node; every node inside the box; no duplicate
          nodes; no inverted or zero-volume tet; no unused node; no
          triangle shared by more than 2 tets; e_vol > 0.
          Buffered cases also: every interior node inside the shrunk
          sampler box; mesh boundary = the box (every boundary triangle
          on a box face, total volume = box volume).
          Cases marked "none" above: no significant wrong-sign
          coefficient in the .stor.
  merge   node and element counts = sums over the eight cases
  field   (only if a field dump exists) max relative error vs the
          checker's formula below 1e-8

  MEASURED, NOT JUDGED (in each case's block on the summary page):

    sep_II/FI/FF_min    smallest d / max(h_p, h_q) over pairs:
                        interior-interior, face-interior, face-face
    ..._pairs_lt1       pairs closer than that, lt0.9 closer than 0.9
    nn_interior_...     nearest-neighbour distance / h, interior nodes
    track_band*         same, median per band of h (radial cases);
                        track_spread = largest / smallest band median
    face_nn_over_h      spacing of face nodes / h of the FIELD there
    face_lattice_d?     spacing along the box edges / h, measured and
                        as predicted from the driver's brick
    wall_gap_min        closest interior node to a wall, / h
    radius_ratio, dihedral   tet shape; dihedral < 5 deg = sliver
    stor_*              wrong-sign coefficient pairs, Voronoi volumes
    log ...             LaGriT's own lines for this run (its negative
                        coefficient count should equal stor_positive)

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
READING THE PICTURE
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  OVERVIEW (top of the summary page, also logs/mesh_overview.png)

  All eight meshes side by side, as in a ParaView crinkle clip: every
  tet whose centroid lies past the box's mid x is removed, so the cut
  face is made of whole tets and the interior shows. Each case is
  shifted along z by its Z_TRANSLATE, as in the merged dump; case 01
  is front left. Dense blue faces are face lattices (buffered runs).
  The second copy colours every tet by its smallest dihedral angle:
  red < 5 deg (sliver), orange 5-10 deg. Pure Python, about 10 s;
  NO_RENDER=1 skips it, and a failure never fails the run.

  PER CASE: one picture per case, up to six panels.

  TOP LEFT    top view of a thin slab through mid-height. BLUE =
              interior node, GREY = node on a box face (taken from the
              face lattice layer nearest the slab). Black = the box,
              dashed red = the shrunk sampler box when buffered.
  TOP RIGHT   side view, slab through y = 0.5. The buffer gap between
              the face lattice and the interior shows here.
  MIDDLE L    nearest-neighbour spacing: blue = interior d / h, orange
              = each face node's nearest interior node / max(h). The
              orange sits near the buffer size by design.
  MIDDLE R    smallest dihedral angle of every tet. Dashed: 5 deg
              (sliver) and 10 deg.
  BOTTOM L    radial cases: each interior node's nearest-neighbour
              distance against distance from the axis, with the field
              h(r) in red. Good tracking = dots just above the line.
  BOTTOM R    face nodes: spacing / h of the field. On radial cases
              with a buffer this is far below 1 (see FINDINGS).

